{
	title: 'Supervised server mode',
	spec: 'hal serve explicitly starts the foreground headless host without a terminal, using the existing exclusive lock, web service, recovery, plugins and version reporting. If a peer (a terminal) hosts, hal serve asks it over the host socket to hand over: the peer restarts as with /restart host once no call flagged unsafe to stop runs, and a short-lived state/handover marker keeps peers from retaking the role until hal serve holds the lock, which then removes it. Only processes on the host socket may ask. A host that is itself hal serve refuses, so the first prevails and the newcomer exits 1 with the reason. While no hal serve runs, peers take the host role as usual; there is no host-mode setting. A broken config.ason refuses promotion rather than ignoring it. SIGHUP does not stop the headless host; SIGTERM/SIGINT stop it without recording user pauses; SIGUSR1 and /restart preserve intentional restart exit 100. Supervision, not custom daemonization, manages lifetime. Tests cover a real headless host taking the home from a peer host, hangup survival, the restarted peer staying a client, and a refused second hal serve.',
